<h2 id="worth-it">Is Thinkific worth it for a beginner?</h2>
<p>If you have zero audience, paying a monthly fee for a course platform before anyone has asked to buy from you is usually the wrong first move. A better sequence for most beginners looks like this:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Start where the traffic already is.</strong> Publish a short course on <a href="/udemy/">Udemy</a> or a class on <a href="/skillshare/">Skillshare</a> to test whether people want what you teach.</li>
  <li><strong>Build an email list on the side.</strong> Offer a free checklist or mini-lesson related to your course so interested students can follow you off-platform.</li>
  <li><strong>Move to Thinkific when you can fill it.</strong> Once a few hundred people are on your list, a premium course on your own site starts to make financial sense.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you already have an audience (a YouTube channel, a client base, a newsletter or an active social following), skip straight to Thinkific. You will keep far more of each sale and own the relationship with every student.</p>

